# Single job — host, MCP, webview

A function does **one job**. The name is that job. When a module grows a second hero (disk write vs MCP tool vs panel HTML), extract — do not keep stacking steps into the owner.

Works with ponytail: extract **a real job**, not a forwarder. One-line wrappers that only call the next function stay out. Thin barrels that re-export split modules are fine when callers should still import one path.

| Kind | Lives in | Owns |
|------|----------|------|
| Extension host | `src/` activate, commands, panel | open panel, register MCP, watch `.cursor-design/`, postMessage to webview |
| MCP process | `src/mcp/` (or sibling) | tool handlers; read/write disk SoT; no UI |
| Disk SoT | shared lib | artboard CRUD, active pointer, generation / hash — no MCP transport, no DOM |
| Webview chrome | `src/webview/` | Svelte chrome + iframe srcdoc; render what host posts — no MCP, no direct workspace fs |

```ts
// ❌ BAD — activate also parses MCP args and builds iframe HTML
export const activate = (context) => {
  /* registerServer + writeArtboardFile + setWebviewHtml */
}

// ✅ GOOD — owner wires; jobs are named
export const activate = (context) => {
  registerCursorDesignMcp(context)
  const panel = openArtboardPanel(context)
  watchArtboardDisk({ workspaceRoot, onChange: panel.postArtboard })
}
```

```ts
// ❌ BAD
export const save = (args) => writeArtboard(args)
const load = (id) => readArtboard(id)

// ✅ GOOD — name says when / what
writeArtboard(args)
readArtboard(artboardId)
setActiveArtboard(artboardId)
```

- **Host** if it uses `vscode.*` or owns the panel lifetime.
- **MCP** if it is a tool handler / stdio server.
- **Disk lib** if it could run without VS Code or MCP (pure fs + schema).
- **Webview** if it runs in the webview JS context.
- Do not put process-global I/O policy in a pure parse module — the write leaf owns that.
- `*.test.ts` covers the extracted util, not the VS Code activate wrapper.
